  "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ is $[-1,1].$ This means that \n\n$$\n-1\\le \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo,  for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=2.$\n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not injective. Notice that $g$ has two roots, $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$ So, we have $g(-\\sqrt{2})=0=g(\\sqrt{2}),$ while $-\\sqrt{2}\\ne \\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- III is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $\u2200\\,y\\in Q,$ we can choose an $x\\in Q$ such that $h(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(x) & =y \\\\ 8x-3 & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y+3}{8}\\in Q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nh(x)=h(\\frac{y+3}{8})=8(\\frac{y+3}{8})-3=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h({x}_{1}) & =h({x}_{2}) \\\\ 8{x}_{1}-3 & =8{x}_{2}-3 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause $h$ is both surjective and injective, $h$ is bijective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\".",
